#speculation(title: [GPR52/HTT Shared Neural Pathway as a Repurposing-Relevant Target])[
*Certainty: 0.35.* The Kerrebijn 2026 fibromyalgia GWAS's strongest signal was a coding variant in _HTT_, with the _HTT_ regulator _GPR52_ prioritized as a causal gene @Kerrebijn2026FibromyalgiaGWAS. _GPR52_ is an orphan G-protein-coupled receptor that negatively regulates huntingtin levels, and it is already an investigational drug target in Huntington's disease (GPR52 antagonists reduce mutant huntingtin). Because _GPR52_ is also highlighted in ME/CFS genetics, a shared neural pathway through HTT/GPR52/DRD2 is a candidate transdiagnostic mechanism (Origin: brainstorm).

*Druggability caveat.* This is a research/repositioning direction, NOT a treatment recommendation. GPR52-targeting compounds are largely investigational with no human safety data in ME/CFS or fibromyalgia, and the fibromyalgia _HTT_ signal may be condition-specific rather than shared (see Limitation @lim:kerrebijn-fibromyalgia-gwas-limitations). No GPR52 agent is approved or appropriate for off-label use.

*Falsifiable prediction:* If the HTT/GPR52/DRD2 pathway is shared and mechanistically relevant, ME/CFS and fibromyalgia patients with high genetic loading at these loci should show convergent neural phenotypes (e.g., altered striatal/cortical signaling on imaging) that track PRS. Falsified if the loci are not shared or show no functional neural correlate in ME/CFS.

*Consequence:* This flags a genetically-supported molecular direction (HTT/GPR52/DRD2) that could eventually be druggable, but it is years from any clinical application and no GPR52 agent is appropriate today.
] <spec:gpr52-htt-shared-pathway-druggability>
